#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Ruby 4 warns "IO::Buffer is experimental" on first use (the Journal's
# fsync'd frames), straight onto the chat TTY -- interpreter noise smearing
# the frontend's screen, not an output-discipline violation. Silencing global
# Warning state is the EXE's call; the library must never do it for its host.
Warning[:experimental] = false

# ONLY from a source checkout, and the guard is the whole of what makes `lain`
# usable outside its own repository. `bundler/setup` resolves the Gemfile it
# finds by walking UP FROM THE CWD, so an unconditional require here means the
# INSTALLED gem, run inside any project that has a Gemfile of its own, resolves
# THAT project's bundle and dies in Bundler::GemNotFound before `lain` gets a
# say. Measured 2026-08-05 against a real checkout of rack: `lain review open
# 2490` could not start, and reviewing somebody else's repository is the whole
# point of the review surface.
#
# `../Gemfile` relative to THIS FILE tells the two cases apart exactly: a
# checkout has one beside `exe/`, and the packaged gem ships none (verified
# against `pkg/lain-0.1.0.gem`). The CWD is not consulted, deliberately -- that
# is the thing that was wrong.
# Pinning BUNDLE_GEMFILE is the second half and is not optional: the guard
# alone still leaves `bundler/setup` resolving whatever it walks up to, so a
# SOURCE checkout invoked from elsewhere -- `ruby ~/dev/lain/exe/lain review
# ...` inside the repository under review -- fails the same way the installed
# gem did. `||=` so an explicit BUNDLE_GEMFILE still wins.
lain_gemfile = File.expand_path("../Gemfile", __dir__)
if File.exist?(lain_gemfile)
  ENV["BUNDLE_GEMFILE"] ||= lain_gemfile
  require "bundler/setup"
end
require "thor"

require "lain"

# The executable. Deliberately thin: parse flags, wire the real collaborators
# together, hand off to Frontend::TTY. Nothing here is unit-tested the way
# lib/ is (see CLAUDE.md), so nothing here decides anything -- Middleware::Gate,
# Tool, and TTY already carry the logic and already carry the specs.
class LainCLI < Thor
  # The Thor-boundary conventions every command shares: map the lib's own
  # errors (all Lain::Error) onto Thor's exit contract -- clean message to
  # stderr, nonzero exit, no backtrace -- and police trailing argv. One named
  # home rather than a copy per command.
  #
  # `include Boundary` carries a CLASS-level effect as well as these instance
  # methods: {included} declares Thor's `check_unknown_options!` on whatever
  # includes it. That is the same responsibility rather than a second one --
  # policing argv is the half of this module's job that {#refuse_strays!}
  # already does for positionals, and the declaration is what does it for
  # switches. It has to arrive by the `include` because Thor's own
  # `check_unknown_options?` answers false for a registered subcommand NAME, so
  # every nested Thor class needs its own; see {included} for the whole account.
  module Boundary
    # An unknown switch is an unknown switch, on every command class that
    # includes this -- which is the whole CLI, and has to be.
    #
    # Thor's own `check_unknown_options?` answers FALSE for any name registered
    # through `subcommand` (thor.rb:363-380), so one declaration on {LainCLI}
    # would be INERT for `epic`, `review`, `survey` and `bench` -- the four
    # commands a stray switch was actually reaching, because each is a separate
    # `< Thor` class dispatched by name. Undeclared, Thor left the switch in the
    # POSITIONAL remainder: `lain survey PATH --permissive` became an arity
    # error naming `survey open`, a verb the human never typed and which only
    # exists because `default_command :open` lets Thor compose one.
    #
    # The hook rather than a line per class, for the reason this module exists
    # at all: policing trailing argv is already its job ({#refuse_strays!}), and
    # five copies of one declaration is how the fifth subcommand class ships
    # without it.
    #
    # `up`'s post-`--` splat is exempt STRUCTURALLY and needs no `except:`:
    # `Thor::Options#check_unknown!` inspects only the extras collected before
    # the separator index, so a forwarded chat flag is not an unknown switch
    # here. `spec/lain/cli_spec.rb` drives that invocation.
    def self.included(base) = base.check_unknown_options!

    # The one command-body shape every read-only report command shares: say
    # the String the block returns, mapping Lain::Error onto Thor's contract.
    def render(&block)
      say yield
    rescue Lain::Error => e
      raise Thor::Error, e.message
    end

    # For commands that OWN their exit status (watch): pass the block's
    # integer through exit, with the same Lain::Error mapping; Ctrl-C ends a
    # tail of a SIGKILL'd (never-closed) session -- clean exit, no backtrace.
    def exit_status(&block)
      exit yield
    rescue Interrupt
      exit 0
    rescue Lain::Error => e
      raise Thor::Error, e.message
    end

    # The one journal-pass shape `consolidate` and `improve` share: `--dry-run`
    # picks the METHOD, so the flag stops at the boundary that parsed it instead
    # of travelling into the pass as a boolean that changes what #report means.
    def journal_pass(pass, selector)
      options[:dry_run] ? pass.dry_report(selector) : pass.report(selector)
    end

    # Positionals a command has no use for. `up` forwards trailing args to the
    # chat window ONLY from past a `--` separator, which {Argv} has already
    # split off by the time this is asked -- so anything still here is a typo,
    # and `lain up /tmp/repo typo` stays a loud error rather than silent
    # pass-through to chat.
    #
    # UNCONDITIONAL, and that is a fix rather than a tidy: this used to return
    # early whenever the invocation carried a `--` ANYWHERE, so
    # `lain up /tmp/repo typo -- --provider x` forwarded "typo" to the chat
    # pane, where it died inside tmux after the session already existed. The
    # `--` gate belonged here only while nothing else knew where the separator
    # was; {Argv#split_forwarded} knows exactly, so this can be absolute.
    def refuse_strays!(strays)
      return if strays.empty?

      raise Thor::Error, "unexpected arguments #{strays.inspect} -- pass chat flags after `--`"
    end
  end

  include Boundary

  # Which project the command line names, as opposed to which one the shell
  # happens to be standing in: `up`'s PATH positional and `chat`'s
  # `--root`/`--cwd`. One home rather than a helper apiece, because both are
  # the same translation and both refuse the same way -- a path a user TYPED,
  # expanded against the shell's directory, and named in its own refusal.
  module ProjectFlags
    # Declared through a module method rather than inline, the way {ModelFlags}
    # and {CompactionFlags} already are: Thor accumulates `method_option` onto
    # the command declared NEXT, so this call goes immediately before
    # `def chat`.
    def self.declare(thor)
      thor.method_option :root, type: :string, banner: "PATH",
                                desc: "Treat PATH as this run's project root, instead of detecting one " \
                                      "by walking up from the working directory"
      thor.method_option :cwd, type: :string, banner: "PATH",
                               desc: "Run as if the working directory were PATH; must lie under the root, " \
                                     "and defaults to it"
    end

    # `up`'s two arguments, cut out of one positional list.
    #
    # PATH shares an argv slot with the first `--`-forwarded flag, because Thor
    # drops the separator before the command method is entered.
    # {LainCLI.split_forwarded} puts it back from what argv really said.
    #
    # The two halves are then treated separately and absolutely: `forwarded` is
    # forwarded, and every own positional past the first is refused. Adding
    # them together -- which this did -- threw away the one thing the split
    # knows for certain, and `lain up /tmp/repo typo -- --provider x` shipped
    # "typo" to the chat pane to die inside tmux, after the session existed.
    def up_argv(path, chat_args)
      own, forwarded = self.class.split_forwarded([path, *chat_args].compact)
      refuse_strays!(own.drop(1))
      { path: own.first, chat_args: forwarded }
    end

    # What `--root`/`--cwd` change about `chat`, or NOTHING. Nothing is the
    # point of the empty Hash: an unflagged `lain chat` keeps
    # {Lain::CLI::ChatLaunch}'s own default factory --
    # `Lain::Project::Resolver.default_project`, the one place a run resolves
    # its own project -- rather than routing the ordinary case through a second
    # construction of the same resolver.
    #
    # `--cwd` DEFAULTS TO THE ROOT, because "open that project" is what
    # `--root PATH` alone means and the shell's directory is almost never
    # inside it. Left to `Resolver#call`'s own `cwd: Dir.pwd`, the common
    # invocation built a Project whose cwd lies outside its root, which
    # {Lain::Project}'s guard refuses with an `ArgumentError` -- not a
    # {Lain::Error}, so nothing between here and Thor rendered it and the flag
    # whose whole design is about naming a path in its own refusal answered
    # with a fourteen-frame backtrace.
    def project_override
      root = directory!(options[:root], "--root")
      cwd = directory!(options[:cwd], "--cwd")
      return {} if root.nil? && cwd.nil?

      { project_factory: -> { resolved_project(root:, cwd: cwd || root) } }
    end

    # A path the user typed, expanded against the shell's directory and refused
    # BY NAME when it is not a directory. The resolver would take a regular file
    # as a root perfectly happily -- it asks realpath, and a file has one -- and
    # a project rooted at a file is a typo, never an intent.
    #
    # A BLANK flag is no flag: `--root ""` is a truthy String, so it would reach
    # rung 1 and die inside `realpath("")`. An empty shell variable expanding to
    # nothing is the ordinary way that happens, and "an empty flag means no
    # flag" is the friendlier reading of it.
    def directory!(value, flag)
      return nil if value.nil? || value.empty?

      File.expand_path(value).tap do |path|
        raise Thor::Error, "#{flag} #{value} is not a directory" unless File.directory?(path)
      end
    end

    # The catch-all is the point, not the `UnusableHome` rename above it. This
    # lambda runs deep inside {Lain::CLI::ChatLaunch}, and only a
    # {Lain::Error} is rendered from there -- so every OTHER way resolution can
    # fail on a path a user TYPED (the `ArgumentError` a cwd outside its root
    # raises, an unreadable ancestor, a `.lain/config.toml` that will not parse)
    # reached the terminal as a backtrace. Naming both flags and their values is
    # what makes the message actionable, since either one can be the wrong half.
    #
    # rubocop:disable Style/EnvHome -- {Lain::Project::Resolver.default_project}'s
    # own note, and this is the explicit-flag twin of it: `Dir.home` falls
    # through to getpwuid with HOME unset and raises a bare ArgumentError, which
    # is neither a Lain::Error this file can render nor a named refusal. `nil`
    # reaches Resolver::Home, which refuses it by name -- renamed here to the
    # same refusal `default_project` gives, so which flag was typed cannot
    # change what an unusable HOME says.
    def resolved_project(root:, cwd:)
      Lain::Project::Resolver.new(home: ENV.fetch("HOME", nil)).call(cwd:, root:).project
    rescue Lain::Project::Resolver::UnusableHome => e
      raise Lain::Project::Resolver::UnresolvableProject, e
    rescue StandardError => e
      raise Thor::Error, "cannot open --root #{root.inspect} at --cwd #{cwd.inspect}: #{e.message}"
    end
    # rubocop:enable Style/EnvHome
  end

  include ProjectFlags

  # Turns the chat flags into the two collaborators the Agent needs a CHOICE
  # about -- which Provider backend, and the Context carrying the model and the
  # sampler params (temperature/seed) that ride Request#extra. A plain object,
  # not a bag of methods on LainCLI: the provider/model resolution is then
  # unit-testable without a Thor instance. It lives in lib (Lain::CLI::Backend)
  # so `bench record` resolves providers through the identical object; the exe
  # keeps the alias and maps its Lain error to Thor's.
  Backend = Lain::CLI::Backend

  # Errors (a Thor::Error surfaced from a subcommand included) print their
  # message and exit nonzero, instead of Thor's legacy exit-0 default.
  def self.exit_on_failure? = true

  # What the REAL argv said, kept because Thor's parsed `options` cannot say
  # it. `extend`ed rather than written as `def self.` so this class's own body
  # stays the commands (`Metrics/ClassLength`); `super` still reaches Thor's
  # `start`, which sits behind this module in the singleton ancestry.
  module Argv
    # A flag written before `--` that took its value from PAST the separator.
    # Its own class because the answer is a sentence about argv, not about
    # anything the command does -- the shape `Up::Cockpit::SwallowedFlag` used
    # to name, retargeted: `--nvim` no longer takes a value, but the socket did
    # not stop existing, it moved to `--nvim-socket`, which does and which sits
    # in the same slot. The guard's reason outlived its subject.
    class SwallowedFlag < Thor::Error
      def initialize(written, delivered)
        super("`--` did not forward what it looks like it forwards: #{written.inspect} follows the " \
              "separator, but #{delivered.inspect} is what would reach `chat`. A value-taking `up` flag " \
              "written LAST before `--` takes the first forwarded token as its own value, and the PATH " \
              "you typed is what gets forwarded in its place. Write that flag with an `=` -- " \
              "`--nvim-socket=SOCKET`, `--session=NAME` -- so its value cannot come from past `--`.")
      end
    end

    # Thor swallows the "--" separator before a command method sees its argv,
    # so remember what followed it. `up` forwards trailing args to the chat
    # window ONLY from past `--`; anything else is a stray positional
    # {Boundary#refuse_strays!} turns into a loud error.
    #
    # The TOKENS, not merely their count. Thor leaves everything after `--`
    # unparsed and hands it over as ordinary positionals -- verified against
    # this bundle's Thor, flags `up` itself declares included -- so `lain up --
    # --provider ollama` reaches `def up(path = nil, *chat_args)` with
    # "--provider" sitting in PATH's own slot, and only what was really in argv
    # puts it back.
    #
    # A count alone is not enough, and the first edition of this used one. Thor
    # SKIPS the separator when the flag before it still wants a value, so
    # `lain up /tmp --nvim-socket -- --provider ollama` hands over
    # `["/tmp", "ollama"]` -- two positionals for two forwarded tokens, so the
    # arithmetic still balances while the PATH the user typed has silently
    # become a chat flag. Comparing the delivered tail against the written
    # tokens is what tells those two apart; see {SwallowedFlag}.
    def start(given_args = ARGV, config = {})
      separator = given_args.index("--")
      @forwarded = separator ? given_args[(separator + 1)..] : []
      super
    end

    # A command's positionals, cut back into its OWN and the ones argv's `--`
    # forwarded.
    #
    # @param positionals [Array<String>]
    # @return [Array(Array<String>, Array<String>)]
    # @raise [SwallowedFlag] when the tail Thor delivered is not the tail argv wrote
    def split_forwarded(positionals)
      forwarded = @forwarded || []
      return [positionals, []] if forwarded.empty?

      tail = positionals.last(forwarded.length)
      raise SwallowedFlag.new(forwarded, tail) unless tail == forwarded

      [positionals[0...-forwarded.length], tail]
    end
  end

  extend Argv

  # `--help` written AFTER a command name, which Thor routes for a SUBCOMMAND
  # class and not for a command declared here. `map HELP_MAPPINGS => :help`
  # (thor.rb:660) only ever matches argv's FIRST token, so `lain chat --help`
  # reached the option parser as an ordinary switch: an arity error before
  # {Boundary} declared `check_unknown_options!`, and `Unknown switches
  # "--help"` after it. The second is the worse sentence of the two -- it tells
  # a human that a gesture they type constantly is not a thing, while
  # `lain help chat` answers perfectly well.
  #
  # Not a courtesy invented here: Thor's own `subcommand` wrapper already does
  # it one layer down (`invoke_args.unshift "help" if opts.delete("--help") ||
  # opts.delete("-h")`, thor.rb:337), which is exactly why `lain survey --help`
  # worked while `lain chat --help` did not. This gives the commands declared on
  # THIS class the same answer, off the same HELP_MAPPINGS.
  #
  # In `dispatch` and deliberately NOT in {Argv#start}: the forwarded tail is
  # that module's whole subject, and nothing about help may perturb it. Only the
  # span BEFORE `--` is read here, so `lain up -- --help` still forwards
  # `--help` to the chat window verbatim.
  module Help
    def dispatch(meth, given_args, given_opts, config)
      super(meth, help_instead(meth, given_args) || given_args, given_opts, config)
    end

    # The `help COMMAND` argv an invocation is really asking for, or nothing.
    #
    # A nil command name is what says this is argv's own dispatch: `invoke`
    # names the command it wants, and its arguments do not begin with one.
    #
    # @param meth [String, nil]
    # @param given_args [Array<String>]
    # @return [Array<String>, nil]
    def help_instead(meth, given_args)
      return nil unless meth.nil?

      asked = given_args[0...(given_args.index("--") || given_args.length)]
      name = asked.first.to_s
      return nil unless all_commands.key?(name) && !subcommands.include?(name)

      ["help", name] if Thor::HELP_MAPPINGS.intersect?(asked.drop(1))
    end
  end

  extend Help

  # The epic subcommands, on the same thin-wiring rule as bench: Lain::CLI::Epic
  # resolves the home, folds the Journal, and returns a String; this class parses
  # argv, `say`s what comes back, and presents the lib's own refusals -- all
  # Lain::Error -- as Thor::Error, message to stderr, nonzero exit, no backtrace.
  class Epic < Thor
    include Boundary

    def self.exit_on_failure? = true

    method_option :mermaid, type: :boolean, default: false,
                            desc: "Render the issue graph as mermaid flowchart source, instead of the text report"
    desc "status [SLUG]", "Where one epic stands: ready issues and remaining waves, folded from the Journal"
    def status(slug = nil) = render { Lain::CLI::Epic.new.status(slug, mermaid: options[:mermaid]) }

    method_option :discovered_from, type: :string, desc: "The live issue this one grew out of, if any"
    desc "add ID TITLE [SLUG]", "Add a new issue to the epic graph, journaled as a graph_revision"
    def add(id = nil, title = nil, slug = nil)
      render { Lain::CLI::Epic.new.add(id, title, slug, discovered_from: options[:discovered_from]) }
    end

    method_option :into, type: :string, required: true, banner: "ID,ID,...",
                         desc: "Comma-separated ids the issue splits into"
    desc "split ID [SLUG]", "Replace one issue with several, journaled as a graph_revision"
    def split(id = nil, slug = nil)
      render { Lain::CLI::Epic.new.split(id, options[:into], slug) }
    end

    method_option :as, type: :string, required: true, desc: "The id the merged issue takes"
    method_option :title, type: :string, desc: "The merged issue's title; defaults to combining both sides'"
    desc "merge LEFT RIGHT [SLUG]", "Replace two issues with one, journaled as a graph_revision"
    def merge(left = nil, right = nil, slug = nil)
      render { Lain::CLI::Epic.new.merge(left, right, slug, as: options[:as], title: options[:title]) }
    end

    desc "queue [SLUG]", "Artifacts parked for sign-off, ready-to-review first (offline, deterministic)"
    def queue(slug = nil) = render { Lain::CLI::EpicQueue.new.listing(slug) }

    method_option :reason, type: :string, desc: "Why -- journaled with the decision"
    desc "approve DIGEST", "Sign off a parked artifact -- journals a terminal gate_decision"
    def approve(digest) = render { Lain::CLI::EpicQueue.new.approve(digest, reason: options[:reason]) }

    method_option :reason, type: :string, desc: "Why -- journaled with the decision"
    desc "deny DIGEST", "Refuse a parked artifact -- journals a terminal gate_decision"
    def deny(digest) = render { Lain::CLI::EpicQueue.new.deny(digest, reason: options[:reason]) }

    method_option :issue, type: :string, desc: "Which issue -- issue_plan and implementation gate one issue"
    method_option :digest, type: :string, desc: "The changeset content address -- implementation only"
    method_option :provider, type: :string, default: "anthropic",
                             desc: "Model provider for an adjudicated gate, built only when a stage is " \
                                   "configured adjudicated: #{Lain::CLI::Backend::PROVIDERS.join(", ")}"
    method_option :model, type: :string, desc: "Model id for an adjudicated gate; the provider's own default when unset"
    desc "submit STAGE [SLUG]", "Put a stage's artifact in front of its gate and journal the verdict"
    def submit(stage, slug = nil)
      render do
        Lain::CLI::EpicSubmit.from_options(options, input: $stdin, output: $stdout)
                             .submit(stage, slug, issue: options[:issue], digest: options[:digest])
      end
    end

    method_option :resume, type: :boolean, default: false, desc: "Finish a landing that merged and then stopped"
    desc "land ISSUE_ID [SLUG]", "Land one approved issue onto the epic's working branch, pushing nothing; " \
                                 "--resume finishes an interrupted one"
    def land(issue_id = nil, slug = nil)
      # The landing lock's "waiting on <holder>" goes to stderr, beside Thor's own refusals.
      landing = -> { Lain::CLI::EpicLand.new(notice: ->(text) { warn(text) }) }
      return render { landing.call.resume(issue_id, slug) } if options[:resume]

      render { landing.call.land(issue_id, slug) }
    end

    desc "finish [SLUG]", "Take an epic whose every issue is done to main as one pull request, " \
                          "then delete its remote branch"
    def finish(slug = nil) = render { Lain::CLI::EpicFinish.new.finish(slug) }
  end

  # The worktrees subcommand, on the same thin-wiring rule as epic:
  # Lain::CLI::Worktrees finds the repository, runs the reaper and returns a
  # String; this class says it.
  class Worktrees < Thor
    include Boundary

    def self.exit_on_failure? = true

    desc "gc", "Reap lain's worker checkouts, anchors and merged epic branches whose work is safe elsewhere"
    def gc = render { Lain::CLI::Worktrees.new(root: Lain::Project::Resolver.default_project.root).gc }
  end

  # The review subcommand, on the same thin-wiring rule as epic: Lain::CLI::Review
  # resolves the source, opens the round, and returns a String; this class parses
  # argv and says it.
  #
  # `open` exists because Thor owns the first word: `lain review help` is Thor's
  # help screen and `lain review tree` is the tree command, so a branch actually
  # NAMED `help` needs a way through. {Lain::CLI::Review} treats a target as a ref
  # and nothing else -- the reservation is the exe's problem, so the exe carries
  # the escape. `default_command` is what keeps `lain review feature/foo` working
  # for every target that does not collide.
  class Review < Thor
    include Boundary

    def self.exit_on_failure? = true

    default_command :open

    # The enum comes off the strategy registry, and that is not tidying: Thor
    # validates an `enum:` BEFORE it dispatches, so a list written by hand here
    # rejects a scope the registry serves without the registry ever being
    # asked. `lain review --scope by_directory` was exactly that failure.
    method_option :scope, type: :string,
                          enum: Lain::Review::Partition::STRATEGIES.values.map(&:name),
                          desc: "Which grouping to open on -- one per registered partition strategy"
    method_option :base, type: :string, desc: "Override the base ref the changeset is cut against"
    desc "open TARGET", "Resolve a pull request or branch, open a round, and present it"
    def open(target)
      render do
        Lain::CLI::Review.new.present(target, scope: options[:scope]&.to_sym, base: options[:base])
      end
    end
  end

  # The survey subcommand, the review subcommand's shape over a directory:
  # Lain::CLI::Survey walks the tree, opens the round, and returns a String;
  # this class parses argv and says it.
  #
  # `open` and `default_command` for the reason Review needs them -- Thor owns
  # the first word, so `lain survey help` is Thor's help screen and a directory
  # actually NAMED `help` needs a way through. Lain::CLI::Survey treats a target
  # as a path and nothing else; the reservation is the exe's problem, so the exe
  # carries the escape.
  class Survey < Thor
    include Boundary

    def self.exit_on_failure? = true

    default_command :open

    # The enum comes off the strategy registry, and that is not tidying: Thor
    # validates an `enum:` BEFORE it dispatches, so a list written by hand here
    # rejects a scope the registry serves without the registry ever being asked.
    method_option :scope, type: :string,
                          enum: Lain::Review::Partition::STRATEGIES.values.map(&:name),
                          desc: "Which grouping to open on -- one per registered partition strategy"
    method_option :unbounded, type: :boolean, default: false,
                              desc: "Present the whole tree however large it is (the /critique ceiling still holds)"
    desc "open PATH", "Walk a directory, open a round over it as it stands, and present it"
    def open(path)
      render do
        Lain::CLI::Survey.new(project: Lain::Project::Resolver.default_project)
                         .present(path, scope: options[:scope]&.to_sym, unbounded: options[:unbounded])
      end
    end
  end

  # The bench subcommands, on the same thin-wiring rule as chat: Bench::CLI
  # assembles everything and returns Strings; this class parses flags, `say`s
  # what comes back, and presents the lib's own errors -- all Lain::Error:
  # Bench::CLI::Refusal, Session::Corrupt, the ANTHROPIC_API_KEY gate -- as
  # Thor::Error, message to stderr, nonzero exit, no backtrace. Anything else
  # (a bare ArgumentError included) is a bug and keeps its backtrace.
  class Bench < Thor
    # The SAME Thor-boundary home the outer class uses: `render` says the
    # block's String and maps Lain::Error onto Thor's contract, so each
    # report subcommand is one line with no repeated rescue.
    include Boundary

    def self.exit_on_failure? = true

    desc "variance DIR_OR_FILES...", "Report determinism/divergence/distribution across recorded sessions"
    def variance(*sources) = render { Lain::Bench::CLI.new.variance_report(sources) }

    desc "record TASKFILE", "Record N live runs of a task file (one prompt per line) -- spends real API money"
    method_option :n, type: :numeric, default: Lain::Bench::CLI::RECORD_DEFAULTS.fetch(:runs),
                      desc: "how many runs"
    method_option :out, type: :string, required: true, desc: "directory the session files land in"
    method_option :model, type: :string,
                          desc: "Model id; defaults to the selected provider's own default when unset"
    method_option :max_tokens, type: :numeric, default: Lain::Bench::CLI::RECORD_DEFAULTS.fetch(:max_tokens),
                               desc: "max_tokens per model turn"
    method_option :system, type: :string, desc: "system prompt"
    method_option :provider, type: :string, default: "anthropic",
                             desc: "Model provider: #{Lain::CLI::Backend::PROVIDERS.join(", ")}"
    method_option :api_base, type: :string,
                             desc: "Override the Ollama API base (ollama arms only; https " \
                                   "required on ollama-cloud)"
    method_option :temperature, type: :numeric, desc: "Sampler temperature, threaded into Request#extra"
    method_option :seed, type: :numeric, desc: "Sampler seed, threaded into Request#extra"
    # Flag name → Lain::CLI::Backend option. One declarative map, so adding a
    # sampler flag is one line here and one method_option above. The BACKEND is
    # what Bench::CLI#record now takes -- the chat path's own single-argument
    # shape (Lain::CLI::ChatLaunch#call) -- so these are the flags that build it,
    # and record's remaining three (`--n`, `--out`, `--system`) are read below.
    RECORD_FLAGS = { model: :model, max_tokens: :max_tokens, provider: :provider,
                     api_base: :api_base, temperature: :temperature, seed: :seed }.freeze

    # One `say` of the newline-joined paths, through the SAME Boundary#render
    # every other bench subcommand sits on -- byte-identical to the say-per-path
    # loop this replaced (Thor appends the one trailing newline), and one fewer
    # copy of the Lain::Error -> Thor::Error mapping. The Backend is built INSIDE
    # the block so a refusal from its own construction presents the same way.
    def record(taskfile)
      render do
        backend = Lain::CLI::Backend.new(RECORD_FLAGS.to_h { |flag, key| [key, options[flag]] })
        Lain::Bench::CLI.new.record(taskfile:, backend:, out: options[:out],
                                    runs: options[:n], system: options[:system]).join("\n")
      end
    end

    desc "plan-sweep", "Shape x density sweep over a fixture plan's scripted runs -- offline, deterministic"
    method_option :plan, type: :string, required: true, desc: "the fixture plan markdown (P1 format, seams visible)"
    method_option :runs, type: :string, required: true, desc: "the scripted runs YAML"
    def plan_sweep = render { Lain::Bench::CLI.new.plan_sweep_report(plan_path: options[:plan], runs_path: options[:runs]) }

    desc "sweep", "Deterministic five-arm retrieval eval (recall@k over the gold corpus) -- offline, no API"
    method_option :k, type: :numeric, default: Lain::Bench::Sweep::DEFAULT_K,
                      aliases: "-k", desc: "retrieval depth (recall@k)"
    def sweep = render { Lain::Bench::CLI.new.sweep_report(k: options[:k]) }

    desc "arms FIXTURE", "Compare the four orchestration arms live over an ArmTasks fixture " \
                         "-- spends real API money"
    # PROVIDERS, not a restated list: the same rule --isolation follows below,
    # so the help text and the resolver cannot come to disagree about what a
    # provider name means.
    method_option :provider, type: :string, default: "anthropic",
                             desc: "Model provider: #{Lain::CLI::Backend::PROVIDERS.join(", ")}"
    method_option :api_base, type: :string,
                             desc: "Override the Ollama API base (ollama arms only; https " \
                                   "required on ollama-cloud)"
    method_option :model, type: :string,
                          desc: "Model id; defaults to the selected provider's own default when unset"
    method_option :max_tokens, type: :numeric, default: Lain::Bench::SpawnSeam::DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS,
                               desc: "max_tokens per model turn; higher than record's, because an arm task " \
                                     "answers with whole file bodies and a ceiling hit ends the run"
    method_option :system, type: :string,
                           desc: "System prompt for every arm. Unset teaches the FILE/END " \
                                 "trajectory format the grader parses; a blank value counts as " \
                                 "unset, since an untaught arm scores near zero"
    method_option :temperature, type: :numeric, desc: "Sampler temperature, threaded into Request#extra"
    method_option :seed, type: :numeric, desc: "Sampler seed, threaded into Request#extra"
    # NO Thor `default:`, deliberately, and NOT chat's declaration copied over
    # (which sets `default: IsolationBackend::DEFAULT`). A default materializes
    # the key, so `options[:isolation]` would never be nil -- and unset is not
    # `none`: unset leaves Arm::Driver's own Arm::NoIsolation, which leases
    # nothing at all, while `none` resolves a real backend over the shared
    # process environment. Bench::CLI#arm_isolation preserves that distinction,
    # and a default here would collapse it silently. BACKENDS in the help text
    # only, so the flag's advertised set and the resolver cannot disagree.
    method_option :isolation, type: :string,
                              desc: "Isolation backend every arm leases its workers from " \
                                    "(#{Lain::CLI::IsolationBackend::BACKENDS.join(", ")}); only " \
                                    "#{Lain::Bench::CLI::CONTAINING_BACKENDS.join("|")} contains where the tools " \
                                    "write, so a run with the tool floor requires one. Unset leases nothing -- " \
                                    "not the same as none, and toolless runs only"
    method_option :journal, type: :string,
                            desc: "NDJSON file this run's grade records and isolation lease telemetry " \
                                  "are recorded into; --isolation leases the workers, and refuses " \
                                  "without somewhere to record them"
    # Flag name → Lain::CLI::Backend option, RECORD_FLAGS' shape: one declarative
    # map, so adding a sampler flag is one line here and one method_option above.
    # Bench::CLI#arms_report takes the BACKEND these build, not the flags -- the
    # chat path's single-argument shape -- so the three that are NOT the backend's
    # are read literally below: `--system` (an override Backend#context takes
    # rather than an option it reads), `--isolation` (a name for a different
    # resolver), and `--journal` (a PATH here, an open Journal there; a String
    # answers `<<` by appending to itself, so forwarding one verbatim would
    # quietly collect the telemetry in a local variable).
    #
    # `decompose:` and `price_book:` are absent for their own reason -- a lambda
    # and a price table are injection seams for specs, not things an argv spells.
    ARMS_FLAGS = { provider: :provider, api_base: :api_base, model: :model,
                   max_tokens: :max_tokens, temperature: :temperature, seed: :seed }.freeze

    # The Backend is built INSIDE `render`, as `record` does: a refusal from its
    # own construction is a Lain::Error like any other, and outside the block
    # nothing would map it to Thor's contract. The `ensure` below tolerates a
    # raise from either side of `lease_journal`.
    def arms(fixture)
      refuse_journal_without_isolation!
      journal = lease_journal
      render do
        backend = Lain::CLI::Backend.new(ARMS_FLAGS.to_h { |flag, key| [key, options[flag]] })
        Lain::Bench::CLI.new.arms_report(fixture_path: fixture, backend:, journal:,
                                         system: options[:system], isolation: options[:isolation])
      end
    ensure
      # Closing is what discards a journal this run created and never wrote to,
      # so a refusal leaves no zero-byte artifact -- the rule the chat path keeps.
      journal&.close
    end

    no_commands do
      # The argv door in front of Bench::CLI#arm_isolation's deliberate CRASH.
      # A journal with no isolation name to resolve it against raises a bare
      # ArgumentError there -- it refuses to silently drop an option the caller
      # asked for -- and that is right for the library, whose caller is a
      # programmer. It rested on nothing in argv being able to reach it, and
      # --journal is what broke that premise. So this closes the door in FRONT
      # of that refusal, which stays exactly as it is and stays the authority:
      # two flags in a defensible combination get a sentence, not a backtrace
      # (the rule at the top of this file -- every Lain::Error presents clean,
      # anything else is a bug and keeps its trace).
      #
      # {CLI::ChatLaunch#refuse_windows_without_journal!} is the same shape one
      # layer down, for the same reason: refuse the PAIR at the door.
      def refuse_journal_without_isolation!
        return unless options[:journal] && options[:isolation].nil?

        raise Thor::Error, "--journal records this run's grades AND the isolation leases it takes, and " \
                           "nothing leases without --isolation: drop --journal, or add --isolation " \
                           "#{Lain::Bench::CLI::CONTAINING_BACKENDS.join("|")} to record both. Naming a " \
                           "backend that does not contain is refused separately, once the tools are known"
      end

      # A SET --isolation REQUIRES a journal, and Bench::CLI is what refuses the
      # pair: IsolationBackend decorates BY NEED, so an unjournalled resolve hands
      # back a bare backend emitting no Telemetry::IsolationLease at all -- an
      # isolated, paid run nothing can observe. That refusal names the missing
      # journal, and the flag it asks for is right here in the same help output.
      #
      # ITS OWN FD, on the operator's own path. Not stderr, which Journal's
      # contract forbids outright and which `render` fills with this command's
      # error message anyway -- one such line and the record stops parsing, which
      # is the wound output discipline exists for. Not Journal.open's DEFAULT
      # path either: that lands in Paths#sessions_dir, which `lain sessions` and
      # --resume enumerate, and a header-less bench journal is not a session.
      def lease_journal = options[:journal] && Lain::Journal.open(options[:journal])
    end
  end

  desc "epic SUBCOMMAND ...ARGS", "The epic tier: artifact home, issue graph, and sign-off queue"
  subcommand "epic", Epic

  desc "worktrees SUBCOMMAND", "lain's worker checkouts: gc reaps the ones whose work is safe elsewhere"
  subcommand "worktrees", Worktrees

  desc "review [open] TARGET", "Review a pull request or a branch: `lain review 4821`, " \
                               "`lain review feature/foo`. Say `open` when the target " \
                               "collides with a command name -- `lain review open help`"
  subcommand "review", Review

  desc "survey [open] PATH", "Review a directory as it stands: `lain survey ./docs`. Say `open` " \
                             "when the path collides with a command name -- `lain survey open help`"
  subcommand "survey", Survey

  desc "bench SUBCOMMAND ...ARGS",
       "Bench drivers over recorded sessions, and the live arm comparison " \
       "(variance, record, sweep, plan-sweep, arms)"
  subcommand "bench", Bench

  # WHO answers a turn, and how it samples: the provider, its endpoint, the
  # model, the token ceiling, and the two sampler knobs that ride
  # `Request#extra`. Its own module on {CompactionFlags}' rule -- one question,
  # six flags that only make sense read together, and one object in lib that
  # already owns the whole resolution ({Lain::CLI::Backend}), so this band and
  # that object have the same boundary.
  #
  # Extracted by the same pressure that produced {CompactionFlags}:
  # this class reached its Metrics/ClassLength budget, and the rule is extract,
  # never loosen. A nested module's body does not count toward the enclosing
  # class's length, so a band moved here is a band the exe stops paying for.
  #
  # Declaration ORDER is preserved by calling `.declare` exactly where the flags
  # used to sit: Thor accumulates `method_option` onto the command declared
  # NEXT, so a band that moved would silently attach to the wrong command.
  module ModelFlags
    # Split in two on {CompactionFlags}' own rule (its three methods are one
    # question each), and by the cop's insistence: WHICH endpoint answers, then
    # HOW it answers. The order the two are called in is the order the flags
    # used to sit in, which is the part that must not drift.
    def self.declare(thor)
      endpoint(thor)
      sampling(thor)
      throughput(thor)
    end

    # Every default here reads {Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults} first, so a project can
    # pin its endpoint in a direnv `.envrc` and stop retyping it. An explicit
    # flag still wins: Thor consults a `default:` only when the flag is absent.
    # See EnvDefaults for what is deliberately NOT env-configurable, and why.
    def self.endpoint(thor)
      thor.method_option :provider, type: :string, default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.string("LAIN_PROVIDER", "anthropic"),
                                    desc: "Model provider: #{Lain::CLI::Backend::PROVIDERS.join(", ")} [$LAIN_PROVIDER]"
      thor.method_option :api_base, type: :string, default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.string("LAIN_API_BASE"),
                                    desc: "Override the Ollama API base (ollama arms only; https " \
                                          "required on ollama-cloud) [$LAIN_API_BASE]"
      thor.method_option :model, type: :string, default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.string("LAIN_MODEL"),
                                 desc: "Model id; defaults to the selected provider's own default when unset " \
                                       "[$LAIN_MODEL]"
    end

    def self.sampling(thor)
      thor.method_option :max_tokens, type: :numeric,
                                      default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.numeric("LAIN_MAX_TOKENS", 4_096),
                                      desc: "max_tokens per model turn [$LAIN_MAX_TOKENS]"
      thor.method_option :temperature, type: :numeric, default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.numeric("LAIN_TEMPERATURE"),
                                       desc: "Sampler temperature, threaded into Request#extra (ollama honors it) " \
                                             "[$LAIN_TEMPERATURE]"
      thor.method_option :seed, type: :numeric, default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.numeric("LAIN_SEED"),
                                desc: "Sampler seed, threaded into Request#extra (ollama honors it) [$LAIN_SEED]"
    end

    # The two ollama runner knobs that are not sampling behaviour -- a third
    # method on the same rule that split {declare} in two, and by the same cop's
    # insistence (`sampling` reached Metrics/MethodLength at five flags;
    # extract, never loosen).
    #
    # The property both defaults must hold: they RESOLVE TO nil when the
    # variable is unset. {Lain::CLI::Backend}'s sampler loop keeps a key only
    # when its value is non-nil, so an unset knob contributes nothing and the
    # payload stays byte-identical to before this method existed.
    # {Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.numeric} answers nil for an unset variable, which
    # is exactly that absence -- so reading the environment is safe here in a
    # way a LITERAL default would not be: a literal materializes the key and
    # puts an `options` object on every ollama request in the process, a wire
    # change for callers that never asked for one. `backend_spec.rb`'s
    # no-options example is what fails if this stops holding.
    def self.throughput(thor)
      thor.method_option :num_batch, type: :numeric, default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.numeric("LAIN_NUM_BATCH"),
                                     desc: "Ollama prompt batch size; ollama's own default of 512 costs ~1.31x " \
                                           "prefill on this box, 2048 is the measured setting. A local-SERVER " \
                                           "knob: it is sent on ollama-cloud too, where its effect is " \
                                           "unmeasured [$LAIN_NUM_BATCH]"
      thor.method_option :num_ctx, type: :numeric, default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.numeric("LAIN_NUM_CTX"),
                                   desc: "Ollama context length for the request; unset serves the model's own. " \
                                         "A local-SERVER knob: it is sent on ollama-cloud too, where its effect " \
                                         "is unmeasured and nothing refuses a value above the model's trained " \
                                         "maximum [$LAIN_NUM_CTX]"
    end
  end

  # The compaction band of `chat`'s flag surface: whether to compact at all, the
  # three byte knobs, and the summarizer TIER. Its own module because it is its
  # own question -- "how does this run manage a growing history", answered by
  # seven flags that only make sense read together, and `lain help chat` is where
  # an operator meets them as a group.
  #
  # It stays in the exe rather than lib/ for a hard reason: the library does not
  # depend on Thor, and a lib object calling `method_option` would make it.
  #
  # Every default names its authority in lib -- Backend's constants, the oracle's
  # ceiling -- rather than restating a number, so `lain help chat` and the wiring
  # cannot come to disagree about what an unset flag means.
  module CompactionFlags
    # Thor's `method_option` accumulates onto the command declared NEXT, so this
    # runs from the class body where the band belongs: after chat's other flags,
    # before `def chat`.
    def self.declare(thor)
      history(thor)
      strategy(thor)
      pipeline(thor)
      summarizer(thor)
    end

    # ON by default: a working harness default, not an opt-in arm.
    def self.history(thor)
      thor.method_option :compact, type: :boolean, default: true,
                                   desc: "Summarize the history's head as it grows; --no-compact opts out"
      thor.method_option :compact_bytes, type: :numeric, default: Backend::DEFAULT_BYTE_THRESHOLD,
                                         desc: "Droppable-head bytes above which a compaction is warranted"
      thor.method_option :compact_cap, type: :numeric, default: Backend::DEFAULT_HARD_CAP,
                                       desc: "History bytes that force a compaction even while the cache is warm"
      thor.method_option :compact_keep, type: :numeric, default: Backend::DEFAULT_KEEP_LAST,
                                        desc: "Trailing messages a compaction leaves verbatim"
    end

    # HOW a compacted span collapses, its own method for the same reason
    # {#history} and {#summarizer} already are one each: three questions that
    # only make sense read on their own. Names
    # {Lain::CLI::CompactionStrategy::STRATEGIES} rather than restating the
    # set, the rule the compact knobs and `--isolation` both follow: the
    # flag's help text and the resolver cannot come to disagree about what a
    # strategy name means.
    # NO `default:`, and that is load-bearing rather than an omission.
    # {Lain::CLI::CompactionStrategy}'s own header says the fallback is that
    # class's DEFAULT constant and "not a Thor default" -- and a Thor default
    # here does not merely duplicate it, it makes the flag's OFF STATE
    # unreachable: Thor materializes the key, so `options[:compact_strategy]`
    # is never nil in a real run, and {Lain::CLI::Backend::SpanSummarizer}'s
    # "no flag named a strategy" branch becomes dead code from the CLI. The
    # eager tier -- the control arm every `--compact-strategy` run is measured
    # against -- would then be selectable only from an options Hash the
    # executable cannot produce. Unset must reach the reader as nil.
    def self.strategy(thor)
      thor.method_option :compact_strategy, type: :string,
                                            desc: "How a compacted span collapses " \
                                                  "(#{Lain::CLI::CompactionStrategy::STRATEGIES.join(", ")}; " \
                                                  "unset keeps the eager tool-result tier)"
    end

    # Beside `--compact-strategy` because it is the other render-strategy arm,
    # and with NO `default:` for that flag's reason above: a Thor default
    # materializes the key, so the unset arm -- the Context every recorded
    # session already renders under, whose header names no pipeline -- could
    # never reach {Lain::CLI::ContextPipeline.named} as nil. Names
    # {Lain::CLI::ContextPipeline::PIPELINES} rather than restating the set.
    def self.pipeline(thor)
      thor.method_option :context_pipeline, type: :string,
                                            desc: "Which context pipeline renders each request " \
                                                  "(#{Lain::CLI::ContextPipeline::PIPELINES.keys.join(", ")}, " \
                                                  "or several joined by +, applied left to right). A word " \
                                                  "REPLACES the default: prune alone sends no reminders and " \
                                                  "no cache breakpoints, prune+default keeps both. Unset " \
                                                  "renders the default and records no name"
    end

    # A TIER, chosen independently of the chat model: a frontier chat can
    # summarize locally for free, and a local chat can buy better summaries.
    # Ahead of both sits the deterministic tier of `.lain/summarizers.rb`, which
    # costs no tokens and takes no flag -- these three only decide what handles
    # what it declines.
    #
    # --summarizer-model carries no Thor default on purpose: like --model it
    # resolves to the SELECTED provider's own default, which is not knowable
    # until --summarizer-provider is parsed.
    # Split on {ModelFlags}' own rule -- WHICH endpoint summarizes, then how big
    # a summary it may write -- because adding the env defaults pushed the single
    # method past MethodLength, and the answer to that is an extraction.
    def self.summarizer(thor)
      summarizer_endpoint(thor)
      summarizer_budget(thor)
    end

    # Its own method only because the two-argument call does not fit inline
    # beside the `desc:` without pushing the declaration past MethodLength.
    def self.summarizer_provider_default
      Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.string("LAIN_SUMMARIZER_PROVIDER", Backend::DEFAULT_SUMMARIZER_PROVIDER)
    end

    def self.summarizer_endpoint(thor)
      thor.method_option :summarizer_provider, type: :string, default: summarizer_provider_default,
                                               desc: "Provider for the compaction summarizer, chosen separately " \
                                                     "from --provider: #{Lain::CLI::Backend::PROVIDERS.join(", ")} " \
                                                     "[$LAIN_SUMMARIZER_PROVIDER]"
      thor.method_option :summarizer_model, type: :string,
                                            default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.string("LAIN_SUMMARIZER_MODEL"),
                                            desc: "Model id for the compaction summarizer; defaults to the " \
                                                  "chat's --model when both tiers name one provider, else to " \
                                                  "the summarizer provider's own default [$LAIN_SUMMARIZER_MODEL]"
    end

    def self.summarizer_budget(thor)
      thor.method_option :summarizer_max_tokens, type: :numeric,
                                                 default: Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults.numeric(
                                                   "LAIN_SUMMARIZER_MAX_TOKENS", Lain::Oracle::Model::DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS
                                                 ),
                                                 desc: "max_tokens per summary; a truncated summary REPLACES the " \
                                                       "result it compressed, so this is sized for a paragraph " \
                                                       "[$LAIN_SUMMARIZER_MAX_TOKENS]"
    end
  end

  # WHAT this chat is asked, and whether anyone is there to be asked back --
  # two flags naming one idea, so a declarer of their own on {SurfaceFlags}'
  # rule (the second one pushed the Thor class past Metrics/ClassLength, and
  # the rule is extract, never loosen).
  #
  # They are ORTHOGONAL and the pairing is not a coupling: `--prompt` alone
  # seeds a chat that then reads the terminal exactly as an unseeded one does,
  # which is what the `/btw` child is built on, and its exit status is
  # deliberately unchanged. `--non-interactive` is what says nobody will type
  # the next line -- so it NEEDS a seeded question ({CLI::ChatLaunch} refuses
  # the pair up front) and, having no human, it denies every gated call,
  # refuses `ask_human` by name, and finally makes the process exit status mean
  # something.
  module AskingFlags
    # The description is data, not computation, and hoisting it out of the
    # method is what keeps the paragraph an operator needs from arguing with
    # Metrics/MethodLength -- {Lain::Tools::AskHuman::DESCRIPTION}'s idiom, for
    # the same reason.
    HEADLESS = "Run --prompt's question and stop, reading nothing from the terminal: gated tool calls " \
               "are denied and ask_human refuses by name (no human is attached to answer either), and " \
               "the process exits non-zero when a turn could not finish. Needs --prompt. Run it " \
               "DIRECTLY, not through `lain up` -- that puts the chat in a tmux pane whose exit status " \
               "nothing reads, which is the whole of what this flag adds"

    # @param thor [Class] the Thor class to declare on
    # @return [void]
    def self.declare(thor)
      thor.method_option :prompt, type: :string,
                                  desc: "Seed the first question (the /btw child's side-question); the chat " \
                                        "then reads the terminal as usual"
      thor.method_option :non_interactive, type: :boolean, default: false, desc: HEADLESS
    end
  end

  # The opt-in surfaces that answer a PARKED approval ahead of the human, both
  # of them meta-agents racing the person the queue is already asking. Grouped
  # into a declarer of their own on {ModelFlags}' rule -- the second one pushed
  # the Thor class past Metrics/ClassLength, and two flags naming one idea are
  # an object, not a limit to raise.
  #
  # They are DISJOINT, and that is the reason it is safe to run both: the auto
  # surface judges only pendings that release nothing sensitive, and the secret
  # oracle only pendings that would ({Approval::AutoSurface} and
  # {Approval::SecretSurface} each filter structurally). Neither can decide the
  # other's.
  module SurfaceFlags
    # @param thor [Class] the Thor class to declare on
    # @return [void]
    def self.declare(thor)
      thor.method_option :auto_approve, type: :boolean, default: false,
                                        desc: "Wire an extra approval surface: an auto_approver role judges " \
                                              "pendings the human hasn't yet -- opt-in, races the human's own " \
                                              "surfaces, and abstains on anything holding sensitive regions"
      thor.method_option :secret_oracle, type: :boolean, default: false,
                                         desc: "Wire an extra approval surface: a LOCAL model (ollama, never a " \
                                               "remote provider whatever --provider says) triages parked reads " \
                                               "that WOULD release sensitive regions, ahead of the human. It is " \
                                               "shown the path and the region count, never the file's contents"
    end
  end

  # WHERE a shell command becomes a process -- the {Lain::Exec} seam's two
  # selectable backends, and the image the container one starts from. A
  # declarer of their own on {SurfaceFlags}' rule: two flags naming one idea
  # are an object, and the Thor class has no Metrics/ClassLength headroom to
  # spend on a second `method_option`.
  #
  # ⚠️ NEITHER FLAG IS A SAFETY CONTROL. `--exec docker` changes which
  # toolchain a command finds, never what the command is permitted to do: the
  # container runs as the calling user on a read-write mount of the project,
  # and {Lain::Exec::Docker}'s class doc says so at length. Tier-3 approval is
  # the boundary for every value of this flag. Help text that started
  # promising confinement would be the defect, not the feature.
  module ExecFlags
    # Constants rather than inline strings, because the two `method_option`
    # calls plus their prose ran this declarer past Metrics/MethodLength --
    # and the sentences are the part a reader of `--help` actually reads.
    BACKEND = "How a shell command becomes a process " \
              "(#{Lain::CLI::ExecBackend::BACKENDS.join(", ")}): in this process, or one `docker run --rm` " \
              "per command with the project mounted. It selects a toolchain, never a confinement -- tier-3 " \
              "approval is still the boundary. `docker` refuses a PIPELINE (a container takes one argv); " \
              "crosses a fixed set (LAIN_*, LANG/LC_*, TZ, TERM, NO_COLOR, CI) by NAME on the command " \
              "line, values kept off it; and is refused at launch for a missing client, before a session " \
              "record exists -- but the daemon is not probed until the first command, so one that is down " \
              "surfaces then".freeze

    IMAGE = "The image `--exec docker` starts each container from " \
            "(default #{Lain::Exec::Docker::DEFAULT_IMAGE}, which carries `sh` and little else -- name " \
            "your project's own)".freeze

    # @param thor [Class] the Thor class to declare on
    # @return [void]
    def self.declare(thor)
      thor.method_option :exec, type: :string, default: Lain::CLI::ExecBackend::DEFAULT, desc: BACKEND
      thor.method_option :exec_image, type: :string, banner: "IMAGE", desc: IMAGE
    end

    # The launch-time half of the flag, in a module method rather than inline in
    # `#chat` on {ModelFlags}' rule -- and because four reads of the options hash
    # spent the last of that method's Metrics/AbcSize budget, which is the cop
    # asking for the object this module already is.
    #
    # Refused AHEAD of {Lain::CLI::ChatLaunch}, so an `--exec` name this box
    # cannot run costs the operator nothing: no project resolved, no journal
    # opened, no session file on disk. The backend it builds is DROPPED, which
    # is what makes `root: Dir.pwd` the honest value here -- the question asked
    # is "can this box run that flag?", never "what would it mount?". The
    # backend a run actually uses is resolved by
    # {Lain::CLI::Wiring::ToolsetBuild} from the resolved Project's root.
    #
    # @param options [Hash] the parsed chat flags
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [Lain::Error] an unknown name, or docker with no client on PATH
    def self.refuse_unusable!(options)
      Lain::CLI::ExecBackend.resolve(options[:exec], image: options[:exec_image], root: Dir.pwd)
    end
  end

  desc "chat", "Start an interactive Lain session"
  ModelFlags.declare(self)
  method_option :journal, type: :boolean, default: true,
                          desc: "Write a durable, replayable session journal (fsync'd); --no-journal opts out"
  method_option :resume, type: :string, banner: "[SESSION]", lazy_default: "",
                         desc: "Resume a recorded session: bare --resume picks the newest; or a " \
                               "filename/prefix under this project's session dir"
  method_option :fork, type: :string, banner: "SESSION@DIGEST",
                       desc: "Fork a recorded session at <session>@<digest-prefix>; the parent is opened read-only"
  method_option :btw, type: :boolean, default: false,
                      desc: "Ephemeral session (<ts>-<pid>.btw.ndjson), reaped on clean exit unless promoted"
  # Thor accumulates `method_option` onto the command declared NEXT, so these
  # run in help-text order and have to stay immediately above `def chat`. ONE
  # statement rather than three: this class sits AT Metrics/ClassLength, and a
  # declarer that only forwards is not worth a line of that budget -- the flags
  # themselves live in the modules, whose bodies the cop discounts.
  [AskingFlags, SurfaceFlags, ExecFlags].each { |flags| flags.declare(self) }
  method_option :nvim, type: :string,
                       desc: "Attach a Neovim frontend to this --listen socket (live lain:// views)"
  # WHICH epic this chat may hand documents to the human from. Unset resolves to
  # the sole epic in the home (Lain::CLI::Epic#resolve_slug, the same rule every
  # epic verb answers), and a home holding several with no name here simply
  # leaves request_review out with a startup notice -- a chat never fails to
  # start over an epic, because a chat outside one has no document to review.
  method_option :epic, type: :string, banner: "SLUG",
                       desc: "The epic whose documents request_review may hand to you; " \
                             "defaults to the sole epic in this project's epic home"
  method_option :windows, type: :boolean, default: false,
                          desc: "Open a tmux window running lain watch per subagent spawn " \
                                "(needs $TMUX and the session journal; incompatible with --no-journal)"
  # Names IsolationBackend::BACKENDS rather than restating the set, on the same
  # rule the compact knobs below follow: the flag's help text and the resolver
  # cannot come to disagree about what a backend name means. Resolved ONCE per
  # chat launch (Wiring#fleet_isolation) and shared between the fleet
  # Supervisor and every model-dispatched spawn's lease (Tools::Subagent#run_child,
  # #adopt_actor) -- one-shot or actor, and a nested spawn (Tools::Subagent#descend)
  # leases and releases its own dispatch too, so a grandchild never escapes into
  # the host.
  method_option :isolation, type: :string, default: Lain::CLI::IsolationBackend::DEFAULT,
                            desc: "Isolation backend spawned subagents lease workers from, per dispatch " \
                                  "(#{Lain::CLI::IsolationBackend::BACKENDS.join(", ")}); the main chat's own " \
                                  "session is never isolated"
  method_option :grace, type: :numeric, default: Lain::CLI::Shutdown::GRACE_DEFAULT,
                        desc: "Grace seconds a first Ctrl-C / SIGTERM grants a run before it is stopped"
  # The chat-side half of `lain up PATH`: name the project instead of letting
  # the shell's directory decide it. `--root` is rung 1 of the detection ladder
  # (Lain::Project::Resolver's `:flag`, exempt from the refusal set, so
  # `--root $HOME` is intent rather than inference); `--cwd` is where the run
  # sits INSIDE that root.
  ProjectFlags.declare(self)
  CompactionFlags.declare(self)
  default_task :chat

  # The lifecycle bracket (resolve --resume, open the journal, run the
  # conversation, always close -- refusal-before-journal ordering included)
  # lives in Lain::CLI::ChatLaunch, where it carries specs; this method stays
  # the flag-parse + error-mapping shell, `say` the one output seam lent in.
  # `--non-interactive` is the ONLY chat that owns a process exit status, and
  # the `if` is the whole of that restraint: an attended chat, and a `--prompt`
  # chat, still exit 0 whatever the conversation reached, because a human read
  # the refusal on their own screen and the status is nobody's signal. A
  # scripted one has no such reader, so the status is the only place a torn
  # turn can still be seen.
  def chat
    ExecFlags.refuse_unusable!(options)
    launch = Lain::CLI::ChatLaunch.new(options, **project_override)
    launch.call { |notice| say notice }
    exit launch.exit_status if options[:non_interactive]
  rescue Lain::Error => e
    raise Thor::Error, e.message
  end

  desc "sessions", "List this project's recorded sessions (newest first)"
  method_option :all, type: :boolean, default: false, desc: "Include ephemeral (--btw) sessions"
  def sessions = render { Lain::CLI::Sessions.new.listing(all: options[:all]) }

  desc "watch SELECTOR", "Read-only live tail of one actor's lineage (SELECTOR = spawn digest prefix)"
  method_option :session, type: :string, desc: "Session file to tail; defaults to this project's newest"
  def watch(selector) = exit_status { Lain::CLI::Watch.new(selector:, path: options[:session], sink: $stdout).run }

  desc "friction SESSION", "Knob guidance from a session's friction signals (offline, deterministic)"
  def friction(selector) = render { Lain::CLI::Friction.new.report(selector) }

  desc "improvements", "Cross-project dogfood notes accumulated via the improvement_write tool"
  method_option :project, type: :string,
                          desc: "Filter to one project: a 12-hex-char hash, or a path resolved the same way"
  method_option :kind, type: :string, desc: "Filter to one kind: knob, bug, missing-feature, or doc"
  def improvements = render { Lain::CLI::Improvements.new.report(project: options[:project], kind: options[:kind]) }

  # The four flags both JOURNAL PASSES share -- {ModelFlags}' rule at the
  # smaller scale that actually recurs here. `consolidate` and `improve` each
  # spawn one model over one recorded session, so `provider`, `model` and
  # `max_tokens` were byte-identical between them and only the two SENTENCES
  # differed; two copies is how the pair drifts into two providers.
  #
  # Not {ModelFlags} itself: that band reads {Lain::CLI::EnvDefaults} and carries
  # `api_base`/`temperature`/`seed`, none of which a one-shot pass over a journal
  # takes.
  module JournalPassFlags
    # @param thor [Class] the Thor class the flags accumulate onto
    # @param dry_run [String] what THIS pass would do instead of doing it
    # @param turn [String] what one turn of this pass is called
    def self.declare(thor, dry_run:, turn:)
      thor.method_option :dry_run, type: :boolean, default: false, desc: dry_run
      thor.method_option :provider, type: :string, default: "anthropic",
                                    desc: "Model provider: #{Lain::CLI::Backend::PROVIDERS.join(", ")}"
      thor.method_option :model, type: :string, desc: "Model id; the provider's own default when unset"
      thor.method_option :max_tokens, type: :numeric, default: 4_096, desc: "max_tokens per #{turn}"
    end
  end

  desc "consolidate SESSION", "Court-clerk pass: distill a session's completed subagent lineages into memory"
  JournalPassFlags.declare(self, dry_run: "Report what would be clerked, no spawn, no API key", turn: "clerk turn")
  def consolidate(selector) = render { journal_pass(Lain::CLI::Consolidate.from_options(options), selector) }

  desc "improve SESSION", "Harness-improver pass: record what would make lain itself better, into the dogfood queue"
  JournalPassFlags.declare(self, dry_run: "Report what would spawn, no provider, no API key", turn: "improver turn")
  def improve(selector) = render { journal_pass(Lain::CLI::Improve.from_options(options), selector) }

  desc "up [PATH] [-- CHAT_FLAGS...]", "Create/attach the lain tmux session in PATH (chat window + status HUD)"
  method_option :session, type: :string, default: Lain::CLI::Up::DEFAULT_SESSION, desc: "tmux session name"
  method_option :socket, type: :string, desc: "tmux socket (-L); default tmux socket when unset"
  # BOOLEAN, and that is what makes `lain up --nvim PATH` mean what it reads
  # like. It took an optional value until the PATH argument landed, and an
  # optional-value flag consumes the next token -- so `--nvim /tmp/repo`
  # listened on a socket called `/tmp/repo` and the session got no PATH at
  # all. A valueless boolean cannot swallow anything, `--no-nvim` is Thor's
  # own negation of it, and the explicit socket moved to its own flag.
  method_option :nvim, type: :boolean, default: true,
                       desc: "Split the chat window into an nvim + chat cockpit (the default); " \
                             "--no-nvim for a plain chat window"
  method_option :nvim_socket, type: :string, banner: "PATH",
                              desc: "Listen on this nvim socket instead of the per-project one lain derives"
  def up(path = nil, *chat_args)
    # ENV.key?("TMUX") is THIS shell's own answer to "am I already an
    # attached tmux client" -- Up#attach_command turns that into the right
    # verb (switch-client vs. attach); see its doc comment for why the
    # branch has to happen before exec, and the cross-server known gap.
    plan = Lain::CLI::Up.from_options(options, **up_argv(path, chat_args))
                        .launch_plan(nested: ENV.key?("TMUX"))
    plan.messages.each { |line| say(line) }
    # exec REPLACES this process, so nothing after this line ever runs --
    # flush stdout first: `say`'s writes above are not guaranteed flushed
    # yet when stdout isn't a TTY (no at_exit/implicit flush fires once
    # exec has taken over).
    $stdout.flush
    Kernel.exec(*plan.argv)
  rescue Lain::Error => e
    raise Thor::Error, e.message
  end
end

# Guarded so a spec can `load` this file to reach the Thor class itself
# (cli_spec.rb driving LainCLI and its Backend alias -- the assembly seams now
# live in Lain::CLI::Wiring) without parsing ARGV or hitting the network. Direct execution
# compares paths; an INSTALLED gem runs through the RubyGems binstub, which
# `load`s this file with $0 still naming the wrapper -- the wrapper's basename
# is also "lain", which a spec's $0 (rspec) never is. Found by the gem-install
# smoke: the path-only guard made the installed executable a silent no-op.
LainCLI.start(ARGV) if $PROGRAM_NAME == __FILE__ || File.basename($PROGRAM_NAME) == File.basename(__FILE__)
